---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import { SITE_DESCRIPTION, SITE_TITLE } from '../consts';
import portrait from '../assets/sean-sobieraj.jpeg';
import shareImage from '../assets/og-image.png';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead title={SITE_TITLE} description={SITE_DESCRIPTION} image={shareImage} />
	</head>
	<body>
		{/* No header bar on the home page: the name in the lead line is the page title. */}
		<main>
			{/* Portrait beside the lead line; stacks above it on narrow screens. */}
			<div class="intro">
				<Image
					src={portrait}
					alt="Sean Sobieraj"
					width={180}
					height={180}
					densities={[1, 2, 3]}
					loading="eager"
					class="portrait"
				/>
				<div>
					<h1 class="lead">
						I'm <strong>Sean Sobieraj</strong>, a cybersecurity data architect focused on making
						security data consistent, connected, and reusable.
					</h1>
					<p class="links">
						<a href="/resume/">Resume</a>
						<span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
						<a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/sobieraj/" rel="me">LinkedIn</a>
						<span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
						<a href="mailto:sean@sean.cc">Email</a>
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<p>
				I work at the Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency (CISA) as a Senior Data
				Architect, leading data management for its Cybersecurity Division. I defined the agency-wide
				tagging and metadata practices, developed a data model built around a handful of domains as a
				practical alternative to an enterprise model of hundreds of classes, and architected the production rollout of the Open Cybersecurity Schema
				Framework (OCSF), which now normalizes more than 100 telemetry feeds from federal agencies into
				a common schema.
			</p>
			<p>
				Just as much of the work is cultural: getting teams to build on shared, curated data instead
				of raw feeds, so an analytic written once can run across many sources and be reused by other
				teams.
			</p>
			<p>
				I started out in the U.S. Marine Corps, maintaining a base network for 35,000 users. A federal
				Scholarship for Service took me through an M.S. in Information Security at Purdue's CERIAS and
				on to US-CERT, where I led the malware analysis team, before I served as a Duty Officer in the
				White House Situation Room at the National Security Council.
			</p>
			<p>
				Back at DHS, I designed and built the malware triage and reporting platform. It cut the time to
				first findings from days to minutes and stayed in use for more than a decade. Later, I led a
				DevOps team building tools for threat hunting, before moving into data architecture.
			</p>
			<p>
				The common thread has been giving security data structure, from US-CERT's first
				machine-readable malware reports to the data models I work on today. Semantic models and
				knowledge graphs are the next step: an explicit map of security entities and how they relate,
				which analysts and AI systems alike can reason over.
			</p>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>

<style>
	.lead {
		font-size: 1.45em;
		font-weight: 400;
		line-height: 1.35;
		letter-spacing: -0.01em;
		margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
	}
	.lead strong {
		font-weight: 600;
	}
	.links {
		margin: 0 0 2.5em;
		color: rgb(var(--gray));
		font-size: 0.9em;
	}
	.links span {
		margin: 0 0.4em;
	}
	.links a {
		color: rgb(var(--gray));
		text-decoration: none;
	}
	.links a:hover {
		color: var(--accent-hover);
		text-decoration: underline;
	}
	.intro {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 1.75em;
		margin-bottom: 2.5em;
	}
	.intro .links {
		margin-bottom: 0;
	}
	.portrait {
		flex: 0 0 auto;
		width: 180px;
		height: 180px;
	}
	@media (max-width: 560px) {
		.intro {
			flex-direction: column;
			align-items: flex-start;
			gap: 1.25em;
		}
		.portrait {
			width: 160px;
			height: 160px;
		}
	}
</style>
